 calculate the total number of such 5-digit numbers. Since we are using each of the digits 1, 3, 5, 7, and 8 exactly once, the total number of permutations is given by:\n\\[\n5! = 120\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the sum of all these 5-digit numbers. To do this, we consider the contribution of each digit to the total sum. Each digit will appear in each decimal place (units, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten-thousands) the same number of times. Specifically, each digit will appear in each place \\(4!\\) (which is 24) times, because there are \\(4!\\) ways to arrange the remaining 4 digits in the other 4 places.\n\nThe value of a digit in a particular place (say the ten-thousands place) is the digit multiplied by \\(10000\\) (or \\(10^4\\)). Therefore, the contribution of each digit to the sum of all the numbers is:\n\\[\n24 \\times (1 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 8) \\times 10000\n\\]\nCalculating the sum of the digits, we get:\n\\[\n1 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 8 = 24\n\\]\nSo, the total sum of all the 5-digit numbers is:\n\\[\n24 \\times 24 \\times 10000 = 5760000\n\\]\n\nThe average (mean) of these numbers is the total sum divided by the number of such numbers, which is 120:\n\\[\n\\frac{5760000}{120} = 48000\n\\]\n\nThus, the average (mean) of all 5-digit numbers that can be formed by using each of the digits 1, 3, 5, 7, and 8 exactly once is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{48000}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{48000}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:592"}
